# Repo TODO — open analyses, verifications, and decisions

<!-- Template: instantiate per Precedent INSTALL.md §1 (practice `repo-is-memory`).
     The typed convention below is the load-bearing part: the type says WHO
     can act on an item (an agent at the desk, a human decision, hardware). -->

Working list of open items that span sessions. Convention: one line each, with
the deliverable/section it blocks and the kind of work (**analysis** =
agent-doable from the desk; **verify** = source-check before external use;
**physical** = needs hardware/vendor/test; **decision** = the user's call).
Prune when done; large completed items get a one-line "done → doc" entry, then
drop off next cycle.

**A TODO is a handoff, not a parking lot** (practice `todo-is-a-handoff`): an item enters this
list only with a stated reason it isn't being done now — *blocked-on* a named
input (decision-owner named), or *out-of-scope* for the session writing it
(then the item carries the context a cold session needs: why, approach,
pointers). Work the current session could finish gets done, not queued; a
sweep that finds an unreasoned open item does it or closes it.

**An item carries a disposition, and silence is the default** (practice
`open-item-disposition`): whether a session may raise the item with its
owner. Write `**Disposition:** parked (YYYY-MM-DD, who)` on its own line
inside the item for one that has been heard and set aside — no session
brings it up unprompted again — or `ask (YYYY-MM-DD, who)` for one the owner
wants pressed. **An item with no line is `wait`**: blocked on someone, and
nobody chases them. Only the person an item waits on sets or clears its
disposition. Parked is not closed; it governs raising the item in
conversation, nothing else.

**Claiming (multi-member repos):** before starting an item, mark it
claimed — append *(claimed: NAME, YYYY-MM-DD, branch)* to its line — and
clear the claim when the work merges or is abandoned. Agents check claims
and open branches/PRs before starting work and warn about overlap; a
claim with no branch activity for a while is fair to challenge.

